--TEST--
An enum in a class or interface body is reached through the owner that declares it, so `Sub::Name` where only an ancestor declares `Name` is `E0405` in a type and in a value, naming the owner that does — `rule:enums/class-scoped-enum`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:enums/class-scoped-enum`: an inherited enum name would give one enum
// as many names as its owner has descendants.
interface Contract {
    enum Speed { Slow, Fast }
}

class Base {
    enum Status { Open, Paid }
}

class Sub extends Base {}

final class Implementor implements Contract {}

// The accepted half first: through the declaring owner's own name.
final class Uses {
    public static function throughTheOwner(Base::Status $status): Contract::Speed {
        return $status == Base::Status::Paid ? Contract::Speed::Fast : Contract::Speed::Slow;
    }
}

final class Refused {
    public static function throughASubclass(Sub::Status $status): void {}

    public static function throughAnImplementor(Implementor::Speed $speed): void {}

    public static function aCaseThroughASubclass(): int {
        return Sub::Status::Paid as int;
    }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Sub` has no enum named `Status`
   --> case.nvs:24:45
%A
error[E0405]: `Implementor` has no enum named `Speed`
   --> case.nvs:26:49
%A
error[E0309]: `Sub` has no enum named `Status`
   --> case.nvs:29:16
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
